# Phantom Pipeline — Environment Variables
# Copy this file to .env and customize as needed.
# These are loaded automatically at startup via python-dotenv.

# Execution provider: cpu, cuda, rocm, dml
EXECUTION_PROVIDER=cuda

# WebSocket API server port
API_PORT=9000

# Logging level: debug, info, warning, error
LOG_LEVEL=info

# WebSocket URL for the desktop to connect to the pipeline.
# Local development: ws://localhost:9000/ws
# Remote: set automatically by `python vast/orchestrator.py start`, which
# writes a wss:// address because Vast terminates no TLS of its own.
PHANTOM_API_URL=ws://localhost:9000/ws

# Set automatically alongside PHANTOM_API_URL. The pipeline generates a
# self-signed certificate on the instance and the desktop pins this SHA-256 of
# it, because there is no hostname to verify and no CA that could vouch for an
# IP that moves with the host. Do not hand-edit: a mismatch is refused, which
# is the point.
PHANTOM_TLS_FINGERPRINT=
# Shared secret for the WebSocket API, also set automatically. Unset means the
# API is open, which is correct for a local pipeline and wrong for a rented
# one — vast/startup.sh therefore always generates one.
PHANTOM_API_TOKEN=

# Playout delay D, in milliseconds: how long after capture BOTH streams are
# presented, whatever the network did in between.
#
# Unset or 0 measures it from the link over the first full RTT window and then
# holds it, as D = p95 + spread + 80ms. That is driven by JITTER, not distance,
# so a link whose p95 spikes during those first seconds commits to a large delay
# for the whole session - and it is often the single largest term an operator
# feels, larger than the round trip itself.
#
# Pin a number here to skip the guess. Smaller is less lag and less tolerance
# for late frames, so look for the smallest value that still holds smooth:
# start at 250, try 350-400 if it stutters. Audio is held to the same number,
# which is why it must not drift - jitter is far more damaging than delay.
PHANTOM_PLAYOUT_DELAY_MS=

# ── Vast.ai orchestrator ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only needed for vast/orchestrator.py. Create a key at
# https://cloud.vast.ai/manage-keys/ — and prefer a scoped one, see below.
VAST_API_KEY=
# Set automatically by the orchestrator on first rental.
VAST_INSTANCE_ID=

# The key the *instance* is given, so it can stop itself when VAST_MAX_UPTIME
# expires. Leave empty to forward VAST_API_KEY, which works and is worse: an
# account-wide key on a rented machine can destroy every other instance you
# own. Vast supports scoped keys, so this should hold one limited to
# instance_read and instance_write:
#   vastai create api-key --name phantom-instance --permission_file perms.json
VAST_SCOPED_API_KEY=

# Where to look, as country codes, in priority order. This is the setting the
# whole migration exists for: RunPod has fifty datacenters and none in the UK,
# and the operator is in West Africa. Widen it only if nothing is available —
# every entry further east costs round-trip time that no GPU can win back.
#
# Priority is STRICT, and it beats price: any host in the first country wins
# over every host in the second, whatever they cost. That is deliberate —
# `VAST_MIN_DLPERF` has already removed every card below a 4090, so the
# remaining offers differ only in distance and money, and distance is the term
# this deployment cannot buy back. If a nearer host is not worth its price to
# you, reorder this list; that is what it is for.
VAST_GEOLOCATIONS=GB,IE,FR,NL,BE,DE

# Pin one host, by host_id from `vast/orchestrator.py offers`. A pinned host
# keeps its IP and its disk, so a resume is warm and the desktop's address does
# not move. The filtered search runs whenever it has nothing rentable.
#
# It is exempt from the PREFERENCES, never from the TARGET. Reliability,
# uplink and the verified badge are skipped so a transient dip does not move
# your session to another country — but the speed floors still apply, at the
# relaxation ladder's bottom rung, and `start` warns if the host has dropped
# below the preferred standard.
#
# That matters because dlperf is measured per OFFER, not per GPU model: the
# live market carries RTX 4090s scoring 50 against a normal 97, hosts letting
# out a throttled or shared card at full price. Pinning a host id and trusting
# it is the same mistake as filtering on a GPU name — both trust an identity
# where only a measurement will do.
VAST_PREFERRED_HOST=

# Quality floors. All are server-side filters on Vast's own published fields,
# so an offer that fails one is never returned rather than being rented and
# regretted.
#
# VAST_MIN_DLPERF is Vast's measured deep-learning score, which replaces the
# hand-typed GPU ranking the RunPod orchestrator carried. Measured in western
# Europe: RTX 6000 Ada 113, RTX 4090 97, RTX 5080 84, RTX 3090 45. At 90 the
# tier is "4090 or better" — the same tier as before, without a table to
# maintain. The reason for a floor is unchanged: accepting a slow card because
# the fast one was busy cost a whole measurement session.
VAST_MIN_DLPERF=90
VAST_MIN_VRAM=16
VAST_MAX_PRICE=1.00
VAST_MIN_RELIABILITY=0.98
# Megabytes per second. The application needs ~0.6, so this is not about
# capacity — it is a proxy for whether the host is on a real connection.
VAST_MIN_INET_UP=100
VAST_MIN_PORTS=32

# CPU clock, in GHz. The compositor is OpenCV on the CPU and is the one stage a
# faster GPU does not touch — ~10.3ms of a ~29ms frame on a 4090 host, ~20ms on
# an L4 host with identical models. Measured across western Europe on
# 2026-09-04, eligible offers ranged 3.5 to 6.0 GHz, so the host CPU swings
# roughly 4-5ms of frame time.
#
# Desktop silicon wins and server silicon loses: Ryzen 9 7900X at 5.7 and Core
# i9-13900KF at 6.0, against EPYC 7663 at 3.5 and the H100 hosts at 2.0-2.4.
# EPYC and Xeon trade clock for core count; this workload wants the opposite.
#
# The floor is deliberately loose — a slow CPU still holds the 50ms deadline at
# 20fps, so clock is a preference, not a requirement, and at 4.5 only two
# offers in all of western Europe survived. The RANKING is what selects on it:
# offers are ordered country, then CPU clock band, then price.
VAST_MIN_CPU_GHZ=3.5

# Effective cores, which is this instance's SHARE of the machine rather than
# the machine's total — a host splitting 64 cores across 8 GPUs offers 8.
# OpenCV will use them, but on 256x256 crops the benefit saturates early, so
# this guards against a starved allocation rather than being a lever.
VAST_MIN_CPU_CORES=8
# Compute capability x 100, so 900 is sm_90. Blackwell reports 1200 and would
# schedule happily against a CUDA 12.1 image, then fail after billing started.
VAST_MAX_COMPUTE_CAP=900
# Rent only machines Vast has verified. Default ON, and this reverses what
# this file used to say.
#
# The old note argued the badge is a datacenter badge rather than a
# measurement, that VAST_MIN_RELIABILITY already carries the measured signal,
# and that filtering on it excluded the best UK box on every axis that matters
# - $0.294 against $0.737, a 5.7GHz Ryzen against a 3.5GHz EPYC, $1.70/month
# storage against $8.30 - for a reliability difference of 0.993 against 0.997.
# All of that is true, and the conclusion was still wrong: it priced the badge
# as a quality signal, when what it certifies is that the machine sits in a
# datacenter. That is the thing which decides whether anything can connect.
#
# Two unverified hosts were rented on the old reasoning, in two countries.
# Both deployed perfectly - CUDA found the 4090, models loaded, the WebSocket
# bound - and neither accepted a single connection on the ports it published.
# The second explained the first: its own record read
#
#     local_ipaddrs   192.168.178.106 ...
#     static_ip       false
#
# 192.168.178.x is a FRITZ!Box default range. It was a machine in somebody's
# house, and the "51 direct ports" it advertised needed a port-forward on a
# home router that nobody had set up. Six check-host.net nodes across three
# continents all timed out, so it was not this end of the connection.
#
# The desktop connects straight to public_ipaddr:<mapped 9000>, with no proxy
# in front of it - that is the point of this migration. So a GPU behind an
# unreachable address is not a cheaper GPU, it is an unusable one.
#
# Turn it off only to shop deliberately among home hosts, and expect to test
# reachability yourself. `offers` shows the badge either way.
VAST_VERIFIED_ONLY=

# When nothing matches, wait this many seconds, retrying the whole search every
# minute. Waiting is free: billing starts when an instance runs, not while you
# queue for one.
VAST_GPU_WAIT=300
# Whether to relax the speed floors when nothing matches at the preferred
# standard. Default ON.
#
# It relaxes in bounded steps — CPU first (cheaper), then GPU, then both — and
# **every step still holds 20fps**, which is the hard target. The ladder stops
# there rather than continuing: the bottom rung is already the slowest hardware
# that delivers, so there is nothing below it worth renting.
#
# This replaced an unbounded fallback that dropped the speed floor to zero and
# could have rented a GTX 1080 — an instance that bills normally and misses the
# target by 10x.
#
# Set false for a measurement session, where numbers from two architectures are
# not a comparison and no pod is better than the wrong pod.
VAST_RELAX=

# Base image. A stock CUDA image: nothing is baked in, and vast/startup.sh
# builds the venv on the instance disk.
VAST_IMAGE=pytorch/pytorch:2.2.0-cuda12.1-cudnn8-devel

# Disk, in GB. This is a cost setting, not a detail. Vast bills storage per
# host and charges far more than RunPod did — some hosts $0.40/GB/month — and
# it bills while the instance is STOPPED, so 40 GB on an expensive host is $16
# a month standing. Size it for the venv and the weights, and check the
# per-host rate in `offers`.
#
# Raise it before a long batch job: video extracts every frame as lossless PNG,
# roughly 4 MB per 1080p frame, about 36 GB for five minutes at 30fps.
VAST_DISK=25

# Billing protection. The orchestrator forwards these to the instance, and the
# pipeline's own timer stops it when the limit is reached — so an abandoned
# session is capped even with no desktop connected. 0 disables.
#
# It STOPS rather than destroys, so the disk and the models survive for the
# next session. Storage keeps billing; see VAST_DISK.
VAST_MAX_UPTIME=120
VAST_STOP_WARNING=5

# Must be unencrypted — the orchestrator loads it without a passphrase prompt.
# Register the matching public key at https://cloud.vast.ai/manage-keys/
VAST_SSH_KEY_PATH=~/.ssh/id_ed25519
# Repo URL for auto-cloning on first deploy — include a token for private repos
# Public:  https://github.com/khonello/Phantom.git
# Private: https://<token>@github.com/khonello/Phantom.git
VAST_REPO_URL=https://github.com/khonello/Phantom.git

# ── Pipeline settings, forwarded to the instance ─────────────────────────────
# Anything set here is copied into the instance environment by the
# orchestrator, so
# `start` produces a configured pipeline rather than a default one. All are
# optional; each is read by pipeline/core.py as an argparse default.

# Face swap model. Also selects its realism profile — see
# pipeline/services/swapper_models.py. Leave unset for inswapper_128.
#   inswapper_128 | hififace_unofficial_256 | alphaface_256
#   ghost_1_256
#   simswap_256 | simswap_unofficial_512
#   blendswap_256 | uniface_256
#
# Pulling every one of them is ~3.1 GB. That is a VAST_DISK cost and a
# cold-start cost on an empty volume, so pre-seed the one being measured.
#
# hififace is trained with a 3DMM in the loop, so the face it generates follows
# the source's *contour* rather than only repainting the target's interior. It
# needs MASK_SHAPE_GROWTH to deliver that — the mask is the target's landmark
# hull, which clips the contour back off — and it pulls a second, small weight
# file to convert the ArcFace embedding into its own space.
#
# alphaface is the one to try against inswapper on identity: it injects the
# source code at every encoder stage rather than once at the bottleneck, which
# is the mechanism that suppresses target leakage.
#
# ghost is the only Apache-2.0 entry. Every other model here is
# non-commercial, ResearchRAIL or unlicensed, which is a shipping constraint
# rather than a quality one.
#
# blendswap and uniface are conditioned on a source IMAGE rather than an
# embedding, so they get the single best photograph (the one the texture layer
# already picked) instead of the multi-photo average, and IDENTITY_PUSH does
# not apply to them — there is no identity space to extrapolate in. An .npy
# source cannot feed them and says so once. blendswap is 1.6 GB, by far the
# largest weight here.
#
# simswap is expected to lose on identity — it is registered because
# simswap_unofficial_512 is the only 512-native swapper, which is a different
# axis. Note it forces 512 compositing on every frame regardless of preset.
SWAPPER_MODEL=

# ── Studio swap backends — for RENDER and photo jobs, never for a call ───────
#
# Whole pipelines rather than a swap model: they do their own detection,
# alignment, masking and blending and return a finished picture, so they
# REPLACE the compositor rather than feeding it. Two of the three swap the
# whole head — hair, outline, accessories — which is the only route past the
# ceiling every ONNX model here shares, since our mask is a hull of the
# TARGET's landmarks and clips the silhouette back to theirs.
#
# None of them can hold a frame deadline. The fastest is ~0.6s per image
# against 50ms, so a stream refuses a set value outright rather than emitting
# nothing while every diagnostic reads healthy. Leave this EMPTY for live.
#
#   reface     image, 512px, head swap. Highest reported identity retrieval
#              (98.8% top-1 on CelebA). ~4.7s/photo at 50 DDIM steps; the
#              authors report usable output at 5, which is the first lever.
#   ghost_2    image, head swap with explicit background inpainting, so a
#              smaller source head does not leave a hole. Apache-2.0.
#   dreamid_v  video, and the only video-native one — it attends across frames
#              rather than swapping each independently, which is what shimmer
#              is. Wants 16 GB VRAM and the Wan 2.1 backbone alongside its own
#              weights, so a render is exclusive of a call on one pod.
STUDIO_SWAPPER=

# Each backend is a separate CHECKOUT with its own virtualenv, because their
# requirements conflict with each other and with ours — REFace carries an old
# latent-diffusion tree, DreamID-V needs torch >= 2.4 with a forked Wan. There
# is no single environment that runs all three, which is why they run as
# subprocesses and why the interpreter is named rather than inherited.
#
# An unset backend reports itself unavailable and the job fails with the
# variable named; it never silently falls back to the ONNX path, because a
# result from a model nobody chose is the confidently-wrong output the guards
# exist to prevent.
REFACE_REPO=
REFACE_PYTHON=
# Optional; default to the locations REFace's own Demo.sh uses.
REFACE_CONFIG=
REFACE_CKPT=
# 50 is published, 5 is the authors' fast setting. Steps is the cost.
REFACE_DDIM_STEPS=
REFACE_SCALE=

GHOST2_REPO=
GHOST2_PYTHON=
GHOST2_CKPT_ALIGNER=
GHOST2_CKPT_BLENDER=
GHOST2_CONFIG_ALIGNER=
GHOST2_CONFIG_BLENDER=
# Kandinsky post-blending over the seam. Off by default: it is a second
# diffusion model on an already slow path, and the seam is judged by eye here,
# so turning it on should be a deliberate A/B rather than a silent default.
GHOST2_USE_KANDI=

DREAMIDV_REPO=
DREAMIDV_PYTHON=
# Its own weights, plus the Wan 2.1 VAE and text encoder it does not bundle.
DREAMIDV_CKPT=
DREAMIDV_WAN_DIR=
DREAMIDV_SIZE=
DREAMIDV_STEPS=
DREAMIDV_SEED=

# ══ MODELS AND WHAT THEY COST TO FETCH ═══════════════════════════════════════
#
# Weights download on FIRST USE, not at deploy, so a session pulls only what its
# configuration selects. This setting is the control over that — for a metered
# link, a pre-seeded volume, or a pod that should never reach the internet.
#
#   selected  (default)  fetch what the running configuration needs
#   all                  fetch anything asked for
#   none                 fetch nothing; use the volume, and say what is absent
#   <list>               comma-separated names, e.g. alphaface_256,gpen_bfr_256
#
# A refusal is never fatal. Masking falls back to the landmark hull, restoration
# walks its registry, the segmenter becomes a no-op — the same paths a missing
# file already took — and the message names this variable.
MODEL_DOWNLOADS=

# ── WHICH MODEL TO PICK, AND ON WHAT EVIDENCE ────────────────────────────────
#
# Ranked on ONE objective: low target leakage, high source preservation.
#
# Read the caveat first. The two dominant leak paths are NOT the model: the mask
# is a hull of the TARGET's landmarks (so hair, jaw outline, ears and neck are
# never swapped) and restoration pulls toward a generic real face. Both cost
# more than the gap between any two models below. MASK_SHAPE_GROWTH,
# ENHANCE_STRENGTH and COMPLEXION_KEEP are the levers that beat a model swap.
#
#   HIGH        inswapper_128            top of the open field (0.73 ID sim,
#                                        96.9% retrieval) AND confirmed by eye
#                                        here. Only model with both. Weakness is
#                                        resolution, not identity
#   HIGH (mech) alphaface_256            identity injected at EVERY encoder
#                                        stage, not just the bottleneck. 98.77
#                                        ID retrieval; best pose-hard CSIM.
#                                        Unjudged here — the one to try
#   TEST IT     hififace_unofficial_256  the only model that moves the face
#                                        CONTOUR. Needs MASK_SHAPE_GROWTH > 0 or
#                                        half of it is clipped straight off
#   LOW         ghost_1_256              registered for its Apache-2.0 licence,
#                                        not for identity. The ONLY permissively
#                                        licensed model here
#   LOW         simswap_256 / _512       0.61 ID sim; its documented failure IS
#                                        face-shape leakage. 512 is for detail
#   UNKNOWN     blendswap_256            no benchmark worth quoting; 1.6 GB and
#               uniface_256              unmeasured for speed
#
# STUDIO tier, same objective:
#   HIGH        reface      98.8% ID retrieval top-1, and swaps the HEAD, which
#                           is the only thing that raises the mask ceiling
#   HIGH-ish    dreamid_v   best quality claims, video-native so temporal
#                           coherence is by construction. No identity number
#   MODERATE    ghost_2     head transfer with inpainting; least identity data
#
# TRAINED tier: structurally the strongest and the least evidenced here. No
# generic-face manifold to regress toward, and a wider crop that moves the
# silhouette at frame rate. Gated on training done elsewhere.
#
# ── SOURCE CONTRACTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Declared in pipeline/services/swapper_models.py, diffed against facefusion,
# pinned by tests/test_swapper_models.py. Each fails SILENTLY when wrong.
#
#   model                     source     framing/form           conv  push
#   inswapper_128             embedding  normed                 -     yes
#   hififace_unofficial_256   embedding  RAW                    yes   yes
#   alphaface_256             embedding  RAW, not normalised    -     yes
#   ghost_1_256               embedding  RAW, not normalised    yes   yes
#   simswap_256 / _512        embedding  RAW                    yes   yes
#   blendswap_256             IMAGE      arcface_112_v2 @112    -     NO
#   uniface_256               IMAGE      ffhq_512 @256          -     NO
#
#   target framing            size  mean/dev        undo on output
#   arcface_128               128   0 / 1           yes    inswapper_128
#   mtcnn_512                 256   0.5 / 0.5       yes    hififace
#   arcface_128               256   0 / 1           NO     alphaface_256
#   arcface_112_v1            256   0.5 / 0.5       yes    ghost_*
#   arcface_112_v1            256   ImageNet        NO     simswap_256
#   arcface_112_v1            512   0 / 1           NO     simswap_512
#   ffhq_512                  256   0 / 1           NO     blendswap_256
#   ffhq_512                  256   0.5 / 0.5       yes    uniface_256
#
# STUDIO tier: source image FILE + target file -> finished picture. No crop, no
# framing, no normalisation — they do their own.
# TRAINED tier: NO SOURCE AT ALL. Target -> dfl_whole_face, NHWC, BGR,
# unsharp-masked first, size read from the export.
#
# IDENTITY_PUSH does not apply to blendswap/uniface (no identity space to
# extrapolate in) or to TRAINED models. Image models get ONE photograph — the
# same one the texture layer picks. The source guards run at upload and are
# unaffected by any of this, as is IDENTITY_PROBE.
#
# ── LIVE tier: swap models, usable on a call ─────────────────────────────────
# Selected by SWAPPER_MODEL above. Sizes are exact, verified by range request.
#
#   inswapper_128             554 MB   strongest identity of the open field
#   hififace_unofficial_256   194 MB   + 21 MB converter; moves face CONTOUR
#   alphaface_256             530 MB   identity at every encoder stage
#   ghost_1_256               491 MB   + 21 MB converter; Apache-2.0
#   simswap_256               210 MB   + 21 MB converter
#   simswap_unofficial_512    228 MB   + 21 MB converter; forces 512 compositing
#   blendswap_256            1584 MB   source is an IMAGE; largest weight here
#   uniface_256               388 MB   source is an IMAGE
#
# All thirteen is ~6.1 GB. That is a VAST_DISK cost and a cold-start cost on an
# empty volume, so name the one being measured rather than pulling the set.
#
# ── LIVE tier: restoration and masking ───────────────────────────────────────
#   gpen_bfr_256    76 MB   default; 5.4ms
#   codeformer     377 MB   29.4ms, and the only one with a fidelity weight
#   gpen_bfr_512   284 MB   37.5ms — SLOWER than codeformer at the same crop
#   gfpgan         340 MB   alternate backend; also needs torch + the package.
#                           No longer fetched on every deploy, which it was
#   dfl_xseg.onnx           occluder; without it, masking degrades to the hull
#
# ── STUDIO tier: nothing is downloaded ───────────────────────────────────────
# The three studio backends are checkouts you place yourself, with their own
# virtualenvs — see STUDIO_SWAPPER above. This setting does not reach them.
#
# ── TRAINED tier: one model per person ───────────────────────────────────────
# Trained elsewhere, hours to days of GPU time on footage of ONE face, then
# copied in. Nothing downloads them and nothing can build them at session time.
#
# When IDENTITY_MODEL is set it REPLACES the swap model and no source
# photograph is used at all — the identity is in the weights, so embeddings,
# SOURCE_BLEND, IDENTITY_PUSH and the source guards are inapplicable rather
# than merely unused. Name the file's stem, without its extension.
#
# It is the only tier that moves the SILHOUETTE at frame rate: its
# dfl_whole_face alignment is a wider crop than arcface, taking in jaw and
# forehead, which every general model leaves at the target's.
IDENTITY_MODEL=
# Where the .dfm / .onnx files live. Defaults to /workspace/identities on a pod
# and pipeline/identities locally.
IDENTITY_MODEL_DIR=
# How far toward the trained identity to go, 0.0-1.0. 1.0 is all the way, which
# is the point of the tier; lower interpolates back toward the target and
# exists so the trade can be measured. Ignored by exports with no morph input.
IDENTITY_MORPH=

# Realism. Normally left unset so the model profile and quality preset decide;
# set them to override while sweeping values against a fixed clip.
# gpen_bfr_256 (default) | codeformer | gpen_bfr_512 | gfpgan.
# The number is the crop the model restores at, and it is the whole cost:
# 5.4ms at 256 against 29.4ms at 512 on a 4090. Only codeformer has a
# fidelity weight, so ENHANCER_WEIGHT does nothing under the others.
ENHANCER_MODEL=
ENHANCER_WEIGHT=
ENHANCE_STRENGTH=
ALIGNED_SIZE=
# Restoration is the most expensive stage and the only one whose cost ignores
# how big the face is. RESTORE_SIZE asks for a smaller FFHQ crop (a model with
# fixed inputs overrides it and says so); RESTORE_MIN_FACE skips restoration
# below a face size in px, shorter side.
RESTORE_SIZE=
RESTORE_MIN_FACE=
TEMPORAL_ALPHA=
COLOR_STRENGTH=

# Identity. The three levers on "it looks good, but not quite like me", and the
# instrument that says which of them is working.
#
# IDENTITY_PROBE measures ArcFace similarity between the source and the output
# every Nth frame, at four points in the chain, and reports them as id_* in the
# REALISM block when the stream stops. Set it to 5 for a measurement session and
# leave it unset otherwise: nothing reads the result, so it is pure cost on a
# call. Read id_out against id_swap — the gap between them is what the
# compositor is charging you for the seam.
#
# IDENTITY_PUSH extrapolates the source identity away from the target's before
# the swapper sees it (0-0.6). Every model lands somewhere between the two
# faces; this moves the point it is aiming at. 0.2-0.3 is where to start.
#
# MASK_SHAPE_GROWTH lets the mask follow the *generated* face's outline instead
# of the target's, up to this fraction of the face's extent (0-0.15). Does
# nothing for inswapper, which does not move the contour; it exists for
# hififace, which does.
#
# SHAPE_WARP deforms the finished frame so the head's *outline* moves toward
# the source's, as a fraction of the measured difference (0-1). The only lever
# here that moves the silhouette at all: every swap model generates into a crop
# framed by the target's keypoints and the mask is a hull of the target's
# landmarks, so measured `outline_shift` is ~0 on every LIVE model at every
# mask setting. This is a deformation, not identity transfer — it moves where
# the boundary sits, it does not generate the source's head — and it is bounded
# again inside the code at 15% of face radius. Off by default and never judged
# on footage; 0.3-0.5 expected, and full strength recovers about a third of the
# difference rather than all of it.
IDENTITY_PROBE=
IDENTITY_PUSH=
MASK_SHAPE_GROWTH=
SHAPE_WARP=

# COMPLEXION_KEEP keeps some of the source's own skin tone through colour
# matching (0-1). Colour matching moves the face onto the *target's*
# complexion, and complexion is an identity cue — this buys some of it back.
# Chroma only: luminance is still corrected in full, because a brightness step
# at the jaw is the most visible seam there is. Bounded per frame against how
# far apart the two tones actually are, so it gives way entirely on a source
# and target who genuinely do not match. Start at 0.4.
#
# SOURCE_BLEND is how several source photographs become one identity vector:
# mean | weighted (default) | norm | best | median. Judge it with
# `tools/identity_probe.py --holdout`, which is the only non-circular way to
# score it — measured against the identity it builds, every strategy wins.
COMPLEXION_KEEP=
SOURCE_BLEND=

# Input guards. GUARD_OBSERVE evaluates and records every guard without any of
# them acting — a session that enforces cannot measure itself, because a guarded
# frame emits the held frame and stops being a sample of the camera.
GUARDS=
GUARD_OBSERVE=
GUARD_REPORT=

# Measurement. DEBUG_FRAMES_DIR writes lossless (input, output) pairs for
# tools/compare_frames.py. Roughly 1.4 MB per pair at 640x360, so raise the
# stride for anything longer than a short clip.
DEBUG_FRAMES_DIR=
DEBUG_FRAMES_STRIDE=
DEBUG_FRAMES_LIMIT=

# Inference speed. All default off, so leaving these blank is bit-identical to
# the behaviour before they existed.
#
# CUDA_GRAPHS changes no numerics — it removes per-kernel launch overhead on the
# fixed-shape models (CodeFormer, XSeg, the swapper) and is ignored for the
# detector, whose input size varies with the preset.
#
# FP16 loads a converted -fp16.onnx copy where one exists; make them with
# tools/convert_fp16.py. It is the largest single win and the only one of these
# that can change what the output looks like, so A/B it with --debug-frames and
# tools/compare_frames.py rather than trusting the latency number.
#
# TRT routes through TensorRT. Engines build in minutes and cache under
# /workspace/trt-cache, keyed by GPU, library versions, weights and precision,
# so each architecture pays that once ever. TRT_GPUS names the cards fast enough
# to be worth the build — anything else runs on CUDA and says so.
FP16=
CUDA_GRAPHS=
TRT=
TRT_GPUS=

# CUDA_STREAMS puts host<->device copies on their own stream so a transfer can
# overlap compute. Ignored where CUDA graphs are active — a captured graph
# replays a fixed sequence against fixed buffers, and a copy outside its stream
# is exactly the ordering assumption capture makes.
#
# ASYNC_ENCODE moves the JPEG encode off the pipeline thread onto the sender
# thread that was already waiting on the network. It also stops encoding frames
# nobody sees: the sender drains to the newest frame, so anything encoded at
# emit time and then overtaken was wasted work.
CUDA_STREAMS=
ASYNC_ENCODE=

# Batch scratch root. Defaults to /workspace/tmp on a pod, system temp locally.
PHANTOM_TEMP_DIR=

# ── Access codes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Base URL of the deployed Cloud Functions. The desktop calls /session on every
# launch and /redeem once the pipeline is running.
#   blank  -> no gate at all (local development)
#   mock   -> gate is shown and answered in-process, no Firebase needed
#   a URL  -> the real thing:
#             https://<region>-<project-id>.cloudfunctions.net
#
# Only the exact literal "mock" (or demo/local/offline) triggers the in-process
# answer, so a real URL that happens to be down still reports as unreachable
# rather than quietly letting anyone in.
PHANTOM_AUTH_URL=

# Mock mode only: shorten the hour so the countdown and expiry can be watched
# without waiting. Ignored against a real URL.
PHANTOM_AUTH_MOCK_MINUTES=60
